1
00:00:01,334 --> 00:00:02,627
"Previously on The Mentalist..."

2
00:00:02,752 --> 00:00:07,048
- Will you come with me to D.C.?
- I wanna say yes. It's just...

3
00:00:07,507 --> 00:00:09,926
Take your time.
There's no deadline.

4
00:00:10,051 --> 00:00:13,095
- Are you going with him?
- To be honest, sir, I don't know.

5
00:00:13,221 --> 00:00:15,556
- What does Jane have to say about this?
- I'm sure he's fine with it.

6
00:00:17,683 --> 00:00:20,937
I need emergency medical assistance
at 812 Ashcroft.

7
00:00:21,062 --> 00:00:23,606
- Bow and arrow.
- What does that mean, "bow and arrow"?

8
00:00:24,190 --> 00:00:26,359
"How long
were you held captive for?"

9
00:00:26,651 --> 00:00:27,944
"I couldn't tell."

10
00:00:29,070 --> 00:00:32,114
"I never saw the sunlight.
Two days, maybe."

11
00:00:32,281 --> 00:00:34,659
- "And you never saw your captor?"
- "No."

12
00:00:34,784 --> 00:00:36,369
My sister's Amy Welker.

13
00:00:36,494 --> 00:00:38,371
She's 16.
You have to find her.

14
00:00:38,496 --> 00:00:40,331
- She was abducted with you?
- Yes.

15
00:00:40,456 --> 00:00:42,124
Her sister was there.
There are more girls.

16
00:00:42,250 --> 00:00:43,709
Forensics is at the safe house now.

17
00:00:43,834 --> 00:00:46,045
They found DNA from 20 different people
on those water bottles.

18
00:00:49,715 --> 00:00:52,260
"And thanks to you,
we shut down a trafficking ring."

19
00:00:52,385 --> 00:00:55,513
- You didn't find my sister?
- No, but we're still looking.

20
00:00:55,638 --> 00:00:57,890
There was another truck. Your sister
may have been on that.

21
00:00:58,015 --> 00:00:59,475
He was just waiting there
all the time?

22
00:00:59,600 --> 00:01:01,769
Yeah. He just makes a point
to be there for me.

23
00:01:01,894 --> 00:01:05,398
- You really like this guy, huh?
- He got a promotion in D.C.

24
00:01:05,523 --> 00:01:09,068
and he asked me to go with him
so we can be together.

25
00:01:15,658 --> 00:01:16,701
"Yeah."

26
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:18,703
Thank you.

27
00:01:19,870 --> 00:01:21,414
You shopping for real estate?

28
00:01:21,539 --> 00:01:24,542
The bureau is paying for Marcus
to relocate to D.C.

29
00:01:24,667 --> 00:01:25,918
I'm helping him find a place.

30
00:01:27,878 --> 00:01:30,673
- Have you decided?
- Decided what?

31
00:01:31,549 --> 00:01:32,925
Whether or not
you're gonna go with him.

32
00:01:33,259 --> 00:01:35,052
No. Not yet.

33
00:01:36,721 --> 00:01:38,764
Well, I hope you stay.

34
00:01:38,889 --> 00:01:41,267
- You do?
- Yeah, I think you'll be bored in D.C.

35
00:01:41,601 --> 00:01:43,978
That's why I should stay,
to avoid boredom?

36
00:01:44,103 --> 00:01:46,564
- What's worse than boredom?
- Patrick Jane?

37
00:01:48,024 --> 00:01:51,277
- You are Patrick Jane?
- Yeah, when it suits me.

38
00:01:51,402 --> 00:01:52,695
I've got a warrant for your arrest.
Cuff him.

39
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:54,322
Hold on.
What's going on?

40
00:01:54,572 --> 00:01:57,325
The Attorney General has found
your coworker in contempt of court

41
00:01:57,450 --> 00:01:58,784
for ignoring a federal subpoena.

42
00:01:58,909 --> 00:02:00,369
- Teresa Lisbon?
- Yeah, that's me.

43
00:02:00,494 --> 00:02:02,913
You've been subpoenaed to appear
before a federal grand jury in Dallas.

44
00:02:03,039 --> 00:02:04,081
- What's happening here?
- Well, hold on a minute.

45
00:02:04,206 --> 00:02:05,249
- Are you Kim Fischer?
- Yes.

46
00:02:05,374 --> 00:02:07,710
You've been subpoenaed to appear
before a federal grand jury, as well.

47
00:02:08,044 --> 00:02:10,921
- Let's go.
- Uh, probably best not to ignore those.

48
00:02:11,839 --> 00:02:15,092
"Two years ago, an arrest warrant
was issued for Patrick Jane"

49
00:02:15,217 --> 00:02:17,470
in connection to the murder
of Thomas McAllister

50
00:02:17,595 --> 00:02:19,472
the serial killer
known as Red John.

51
00:02:19,597 --> 00:02:23,059
Is it true you were part
of an international FBI manhunt

52
00:02:23,184 --> 00:02:25,770
- to bring Patrick Jane to justice?
- That's accurate.

53
00:02:25,936 --> 00:02:29,231
And yet now, you work for a unit
of the FBI that employs him.

54
00:02:29,357 --> 00:02:31,275
I am a part of that unit, yes.

55
00:02:31,442 --> 00:02:33,653
The FBI conducts
a two-year manhunt

56
00:02:33,778 --> 00:02:37,573
finds its suspect, and brings him back
but instead of a trial by his peers

57
00:02:37,698 --> 00:02:40,868
he is given a government job
with benefits.

58
00:02:41,243 --> 00:02:43,120
- Why?
- We made a deal with him.

59
00:02:43,245 --> 00:02:45,331
- What were the terms of that deal?
- I don't know.

60
00:02:45,456 --> 00:02:47,583
He was already a part
of the team when I was hired.

61
00:02:47,708 --> 00:02:50,211
Mr. Jane is required to assist us
in cases

62
00:02:50,336 --> 00:02:52,880
in exchange for immunity
from prosecution.

63
00:02:53,005 --> 00:02:55,257
You worked very closely
with Mr. Jane in California.

64
00:02:55,508 --> 00:02:57,510
Does he confide in you?
You close?

65
00:02:58,010 --> 00:02:59,136
You could say that.

66
00:02:59,261 --> 00:03:01,472
Did he tell you
he killed Thomas McAllister?

67
00:03:01,597 --> 00:03:02,890
Not specifically, no.

68
00:03:03,015 --> 00:03:04,767
Did he tell you
he killed Thomas McAllister?

69
00:03:05,643 --> 00:03:06,852
I plead the fifth.

70
00:03:07,436 --> 00:03:10,231
Did he tell you where he was on the day
McAllister was murdered?

71
00:03:10,981 --> 00:03:12,108
I plead the fifth.

72
00:03:12,525 --> 00:03:13,901
Agent Lisbon

73
00:03:14,485 --> 00:03:18,864
are you besmirching your badge
and refusing to answer to protect yourself

74
00:03:18,989 --> 00:03:20,574
or to protect Patrick Jane?

75
00:03:22,660 --> 00:03:23,911
Go to hell.

76
00:03:39,633 --> 00:03:42,428
This is where we found Daniela,
the second trafficking victim.

77
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:45,013
They separated her
from the rest of the group.

78
00:03:45,514 --> 00:03:47,015
- Any idea why yet?
- No.

79
00:03:47,141 --> 00:03:48,726
The others were being held
inside the house.

80
00:03:48,851 --> 00:03:50,060
It isn't much better.

81
00:03:50,185 --> 00:03:52,229
Now, the girls were abducted
from across the western United States

82
00:03:52,354 --> 00:03:54,022
and transported here by truck.

83
00:03:54,148 --> 00:03:57,234
We believe the traffickers used this house
as a way station of sorts.

84
00:04:06,285 --> 00:04:08,454
It's hard to believe 30 women
were crammed in here.

85
00:04:08,579 --> 00:04:11,039
Took two days to process...
A lotta hair and fibers floating around.

86
00:04:11,165 --> 00:04:13,959
Yeah, I'll bet. Hey.
Can we get some light in here?

87
00:04:14,668 --> 00:04:16,462
We're still looking for the second truck
full of victims

88
00:04:16,587 --> 00:04:18,088
but I think
it's south of the border by now.

89
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:20,382
{\an8}What about the truckload
that we did find?

90
00:04:21,008 --> 00:04:22,885
{\an8}Those girls provide
any useful information?

91
00:04:23,010 --> 00:04:24,511
{\an8}All they could tell us
about was Jesse.

92
00:04:24,636 --> 00:04:26,764
The dead man who lured them
with the modeling scam.

93
00:04:27,097 --> 00:04:28,807
- Ringleaders remain a mystery.
- Okay.

94
00:04:28,932 --> 00:04:30,601
Well, what about the girl that died?

95
00:04:30,893 --> 00:04:33,687
She was trying to tell Jane something
about a bow and arrow.

96
00:04:33,812 --> 00:04:34,897
{\an8}We don't know
what that means yet.

97
00:04:35,022 --> 00:04:37,316
{\an8}I'm hoping for more answers
once we analyze all the evidence.

98
00:04:37,608 --> 00:04:41,028
{\an8}Okay.
Well, you finish up here

99
00:04:41,528 --> 00:04:43,989
{\an8}- and I'll see you back at headquarters.
- All right.

100
00:04:51,038 --> 00:04:53,290
{\an8}- We need to talk.
- In my office. What's going on?

101
00:04:53,415 --> 00:04:55,626
{\an8}Lisbon and I just got back
from the grand jury hearing in Dallas.

102
00:04:55,751 --> 00:04:57,336
{\an8}They're trying to indict Jane
for murder.

103
00:04:57,795 --> 00:05:00,088
{\an8}- Thomas McAllister.
- They asked us point-blank

104
00:05:00,214 --> 00:05:02,090
whether or not Jane had ever
confessed to killing him.

105
00:05:02,216 --> 00:05:03,592
And what did you say to them?

106
00:05:04,176 --> 00:05:07,054
- What are you doing here?
- Uh, I'm out on "bail".

107
00:05:07,304 --> 00:05:10,182
- W-what does "bail" mean?
- It was a con.

108
00:05:10,307 --> 00:05:12,893
Uh, technically the term is "sting".

109
00:05:13,018 --> 00:05:15,771
- Operation jury-scam.
- You're kidding, right?

110
00:05:15,896 --> 00:05:18,148
{\an8}I am very sorry for the deception

111
00:05:18,315 --> 00:05:20,734
{\an8}but to maintain authenticity,
we couldn't inform you

112
00:05:20,859 --> 00:05:22,277
{\an8}until after you sat with the jury.

113
00:05:23,111 --> 00:05:25,906
{\an8}Our target for the sting
is this man...

114
00:05:26,365 --> 00:05:29,451
John Acardo.
He's a young mob boss.

115
00:05:29,701 --> 00:05:32,204
{\an8}He owns the illegal
sports-betting market in Dallas.

116
00:05:32,329 --> 00:05:34,873
{\an8}You guys didn't answer my question.
Did anyone narc on me?

117
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:37,209
How does indicting Jane
nail Acardo?

118
00:05:37,417 --> 00:05:39,920
{\an8}This... is Elijah Macy.

119
00:05:40,796 --> 00:05:45,050
He created a sports-betting website
that was cutting into Acardo's profits.

120
00:05:45,676 --> 00:05:49,888
{\an8}Now, we think that Acardo
had him killed five weeks ago.

121
00:05:50,013 --> 00:05:51,056
{\an8}What's the evidence?

122
00:05:51,181 --> 00:05:54,184
{\an8}Well, it's circumstantial
but more than enough to indict.

123
00:05:54,309 --> 00:05:58,939
{\an8}And then, surprise, surprise the grand
jury voted not to prosecute last week.

124
00:05:59,106 --> 00:06:00,566
{\an8}You think Acardo got to them.

125
00:06:00,691 --> 00:06:02,734
{\an8}All they'd have to do
is sway two or three jurors.

126
00:06:02,860 --> 00:06:05,153
{\an8}And that's what the Attorney General
thinks happened.

127
00:06:05,445 --> 00:06:06,989
{\an8}Now he's asking for our help.

128
00:06:07,114 --> 00:06:10,033
{\an8}Fortunately, the same jurors
are impaneled for another month.

129
00:06:10,158 --> 00:06:12,411
{\an8}And, under the pretense
of a murder investigation

130
00:06:12,536 --> 00:06:14,997
{\an8}I will face them and determine
which ones were bribed.

131
00:06:15,122 --> 00:06:16,498
{\an8}Or intimidated.

132
00:06:16,874 --> 00:06:19,918
{\an8}I can't believe you got a prosecutor
to take part in the con.

133
00:06:20,043 --> 00:06:22,170
{\an8}It's not a con. It's a sting.
Sting.

134
00:06:22,296 --> 00:06:24,756
{\an8}The prosecutor doesn't know
that this is not real.

135
00:06:25,090 --> 00:06:26,466
{\an8}The Attorney General does.

136
00:06:31,346 --> 00:06:33,348
{\an8}- This is the last of it.
- Thanks.

137
00:06:49,406 --> 00:06:52,159
{\an8}Is everyone out?
Locking up now.

138
00:06:55,746 --> 00:06:56,914
FBI!

139
00:06:59,207 --> 00:07:00,334
Daniela.

140
00:07:04,588 --> 00:07:06,715
- How'd you get in here?
- The window.

141
00:07:08,508 --> 00:07:11,261
{\an8}- They released you from the hospital?
- I released myself.

142
00:07:11,386 --> 00:07:14,097
- This is an active crime scene.
- I'm looking for my sister.

143
00:07:14,222 --> 00:07:15,974
We're doing everything we can
to find Amy.

144
00:07:16,099 --> 00:07:17,643
But you haven't found her,
have you?

145
00:07:18,727 --> 00:07:20,145
Daniela, why'd you come back here?

146
00:07:20,854 --> 00:07:22,981
You told us you didn't know
if Amy was even at this house.

147
00:07:23,106 --> 00:07:24,149
I didn't...

148
00:07:24,983 --> 00:07:26,109
but I do now.

149
00:07:31,949 --> 00:07:33,408
This is her tag.

150
00:07:35,494 --> 00:07:36,995
She left it everywhere.

151
00:07:44,753 --> 00:07:45,963
She was here.

152
00:07:52,678 --> 00:07:53,887
Hey. Look at me.

153
00:07:54,721 --> 00:07:56,056
We're gonna find her.

154
00:08:00,936 --> 00:08:02,688
It is none of your business
what I told the jury.

155
00:08:02,896 --> 00:08:04,815
- I did what I had to do.
- What about you, Lisbon?

156
00:08:04,940 --> 00:08:06,984
You snitch on me, huh?
Be honest.

157
00:08:07,109 --> 00:08:09,277
You wanna know what I said,
read the transcripts.

158
00:08:09,611 --> 00:08:11,405
Hey. What did I do?

159
00:08:11,947 --> 00:08:14,950
You shoulda told me this whole
courtroom charade was a charade.

160
00:08:15,075 --> 00:08:16,660
Since when am I a target
of one of your cons?

161
00:08:16,785 --> 00:08:17,953
It wasn't a con.

162
00:08:18,078 --> 00:08:19,997
I don't care.
You shoulda told me.

163
00:08:20,539 --> 00:08:21,832
I thought that you...

164
00:08:24,001 --> 00:08:25,043
You know what?
Forget about it.

165
00:08:25,168 --> 00:08:27,629
Operation jury-scam,
it was Abbott's idea.

166
00:08:27,754 --> 00:08:29,631
He ordered me
not to tell anyone.

167
00:08:29,756 --> 00:08:31,842
Oh, you picked a great time
to start following orders.

168
00:08:31,967 --> 00:08:34,011
- Well, I-I have to do...
- Am I interrupting?

169
00:08:34,136 --> 00:08:36,888
No, it's perfect timing.
I am more than ready for lunch.

170
00:08:37,014 --> 00:08:39,349
I heard about your grand jury.
I'm pulling for you.

171
00:08:39,474 --> 00:08:41,560
I think you did what any good father
and husband would do.

172
00:08:41,685 --> 00:08:44,438
Yes, Jane has all of our sympathies.

173
00:08:44,938 --> 00:08:46,231
Let's go.

174
00:08:50,277 --> 00:08:52,237
How long are you gonna let that go?

175
00:08:52,696 --> 00:08:53,780
What?

176
00:08:54,990 --> 00:08:56,992
Well, you know he's gonna take her
to Washington, right?

177
00:08:57,117 --> 00:08:59,161
Listen, she'll do
what she wants to do.

178
00:09:00,704 --> 00:09:04,416
You know, I've collared
lots of con men over the years

179
00:09:04,541 --> 00:09:05,834
best in the business.

180
00:09:06,585 --> 00:09:08,503
You know what led
to their downfall ultimately?

181
00:09:08,628 --> 00:09:10,672
Yeah. They start believing
their own con.

182
00:09:11,256 --> 00:09:13,967
That's not gonna happen.
There is no con here.

183
00:09:14,468 --> 00:09:15,719
- No?
- No.

184
00:09:17,429 --> 00:09:18,680
All right, then.

185
00:09:29,983 --> 00:09:33,195
Mr. Jane, you understand
you're under a waiver of immunity.

186
00:09:33,320 --> 00:09:35,405
- Yes, ma'am.
- What does that mean to you?

187
00:09:35,655 --> 00:09:38,825
Uh, it means that anything I say
may be held against me.

188
00:09:39,367 --> 00:09:40,577
Very good.

189
00:09:40,702 --> 00:09:42,162
In a moment,
I'll open the floor up to you

190
00:09:42,287 --> 00:09:44,122
so you can provide
a narrative of events

191
00:09:44,247 --> 00:09:46,416
to explain what you were doing
and where you were

192
00:09:46,541 --> 00:09:49,252
on the date and time
of Thomas McAllister's murder.

193
00:09:49,377 --> 00:09:50,545
Objection. You said murder.

194
00:09:50,670 --> 00:09:53,673
Thomas McAllister may have shot
and strangled himself.

195
00:09:53,799 --> 00:09:57,260
There are no objections here, Mr. Jane.
No judge, just a friendly inquiry.

196
00:09:57,469 --> 00:09:58,845
But I take your point.

197
00:09:58,970 --> 00:10:02,682
Do you recall the day
of McAllister's death?

198
00:10:02,808 --> 00:10:04,434
I do, big day.

199
00:10:06,144 --> 00:10:08,146
I had been chasing the man
for eight years.

200
00:10:10,732 --> 00:10:13,860
Any of you know what it's like
to be driven by a moral imperative?

201
00:10:13,985 --> 00:10:15,028
You do.

202
00:10:15,445 --> 00:10:19,407
And you. And you do.
You all have strong jaw muscles.

203
00:10:19,533 --> 00:10:21,451
It's a sign of strength and character.

204
00:10:21,576 --> 00:10:24,788
You, I'm not so sure.
Your eyes are a tad close to each other.

205
00:10:24,913 --> 00:10:27,040
Excuse me.
What are you doing?

206
00:10:27,165 --> 00:10:30,752
I'm establishing
a narrative of events, as you asked.

207
00:10:30,877 --> 00:10:34,381
- So stick to the narrative.
- Narrative, yes, I do love a story.

208
00:10:34,506 --> 00:10:37,717
As do you. You read a great deal
of fiction, don't you?

209
00:10:37,843 --> 00:10:39,928
- Romantic novels.
- That's right.

210
00:10:40,053 --> 00:10:42,514
Yes, an illicit engagement
wouldn't scare you, would it?

211
00:10:42,639 --> 00:10:43,890
Where are you going with this?

212
00:10:44,015 --> 00:10:45,767
I'm trying to tell my story,
if you'll just let me.

213
00:10:45,892 --> 00:10:48,186
- You haven't even started it.
- Well, they seem engaged.

214
00:10:48,311 --> 00:10:52,065
All of them except this gentleman here
who's preoccupied by his new jacket.

215
00:10:52,190 --> 00:10:53,942
That's a little vain and childish,
don't you think?

216
00:10:54,067 --> 00:10:55,944
This is serious business.
Pay attention.

217
00:10:56,403 --> 00:10:59,030
- I'm sorry. I was just trying to...
- Sir, you will not indulge him.

218
00:10:59,156 --> 00:11:00,824
Well, he's happy to indulge himself.

219
00:11:00,949 --> 00:11:02,951
T-that should definitely
be on the record.

220
00:11:03,076 --> 00:11:06,204
Mr. Jane, this is not a forum
for you to chat with the jurors.

221
00:11:06,329 --> 00:11:08,123
- Well, it should be.
- Mr. Jane.

222
00:11:08,248 --> 00:11:10,292
They just seem like good,
honest, friendly people

223
00:11:10,417 --> 00:11:12,502
all of them except that gentleman
on the end there.

224
00:11:12,627 --> 00:11:14,796
You know, you really should
mend things with your son.

225
00:11:14,921 --> 00:11:18,133
All this anger, this bitterness,
this resentment that you're bottling

226
00:11:18,258 --> 00:11:21,595
- it's ruining both your lives.
- Stop now.

227
00:11:22,262 --> 00:11:24,306
You do not dictate the agenda here.

228
00:11:24,556 --> 00:11:26,308
- Who does?
- I do.

229
00:11:26,850 --> 00:11:30,103
This is my courtroom,
and it runs the way I say.

230
00:11:30,228 --> 00:11:31,605
Is that clear?

231
00:11:32,105 --> 00:11:34,566
It is now. Thank you.

232
00:11:41,304 --> 00:11:43,723
- Lydia Faulk?
- Socialized narcissist.

233
00:11:43,848 --> 00:11:45,725
There is no one in that courtroom
keeping her in check.

234
00:11:45,950 --> 00:11:48,244
John Acardo didn't pay off jurors
to avoid prosecution.

235
00:11:48,369 --> 00:11:49,954
He just paid off the prosecutor.

236
00:11:51,289 --> 00:11:54,459
Well, theoretically
a prosecutor could throw an investigation

237
00:11:54,584 --> 00:11:56,628
especially one with circumstantial evidence.

238
00:11:57,253 --> 00:11:58,463
I'll obtain the transcript.

239
00:11:59,756 --> 00:12:01,925
Faulk's too smart to exclude evidence.

240
00:12:02,050 --> 00:12:04,510
It would be in the way
she presented the case.

241
00:12:05,428 --> 00:12:07,597
Halfheartedly, lacking confidence.

242
00:12:07,722 --> 00:12:09,974
jury would see defeat
in her eyes and vote accordingly.

243
00:12:10,099 --> 00:12:12,018
- So, what do you propose?
- A new strategy.

244
00:12:12,268 --> 00:12:15,563
We need to prove that John Acardo
is guilty of murder ourselves.

245
00:12:17,941 --> 00:12:21,152
Jane believes that Acardo
didn't get to the jury.

246
00:12:22,070 --> 00:12:24,239
He got to Faulk, the prosecutor.

247
00:12:24,364 --> 00:12:26,783
- So now we have two targets.
- That's right.

248
00:12:27,033 --> 00:12:28,952
And to take 'em down,
we're gonna have to find proof

249
00:12:29,077 --> 00:12:31,746
that Acardo was behind
the Elijah Macy shooting.

250
00:12:31,871 --> 00:12:33,206
What evidence do we have?

251
00:12:33,331 --> 00:12:35,875
Earlier this year,
one of Acardo's men was arrested

252
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,586
turned state's evidence for immunity
from a drug charge.

253
00:12:38,711 --> 00:12:41,965
He also testified that his boss
order the hit on Macy.

254
00:12:43,049 --> 00:12:46,094
Multiple witnesses heard Acardo
threaten to have Macy killed

255
00:12:46,219 --> 00:12:48,471
if he didn't shut down
his sports-betting website.

256
00:12:48,596 --> 00:12:50,390
But we don't have any physical evidence.

257
00:12:50,515 --> 00:12:52,475
Not yet. But that's what
we're gonna have to find.

258
00:12:52,600 --> 00:12:55,395
Well, according to the transcript
Acardo claims he was at a place

259
00:12:55,520 --> 00:12:58,231
called Il Tavolo Bianco
the night of the murder.

260
00:12:58,356 --> 00:13:00,858
That's his favorite restaurant,
obviously his alibi.

261
00:13:00,984 --> 00:13:04,946
Yeah, but he drones on and on about it
after it was already in the record.

262
00:13:05,071 --> 00:13:08,366
He keeps talking about the restaurant
because he's hiding evidence of his guilt.

263
00:13:08,616 --> 00:13:09,867
You sound like Jane.

264
00:13:09,993 --> 00:13:11,911
Well, you spend enough time
with someone...

265
00:13:13,121 --> 00:13:16,874
Lisbon, why don't you check out
this Il Tavolo Bianco?

266
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:20,169
And take Jane with you.
You two have a nice dinner on the FBI.

267
00:13:20,712 --> 00:13:21,963
Yeah, sure.

268
00:13:30,722 --> 00:13:32,140
Hey. How are you?

269
00:13:33,683 --> 00:13:37,103
All they've got me doing
is staring at photos of sex traffickers.

270
00:13:38,438 --> 00:13:39,564
I need a shower.

271
00:13:40,440 --> 00:13:41,482
Here you go.

272
00:13:43,192 --> 00:13:45,737
- You're right-handed.
- Uh, yes.

273
00:13:45,862 --> 00:13:48,156
So, that heart on your hand,
y-you didn't draw that?

274
00:13:48,448 --> 00:13:50,616
Uh, no. They did.

275
00:13:50,742 --> 00:13:51,993
Yeah, we don't know what that means.

276
00:13:52,118 --> 00:13:54,370
None of the other girls
we rescued had that mark.

277
00:13:54,495 --> 00:13:56,331
- Guess I'm special.
- Jane.

278
00:13:58,791 --> 00:14:00,001
Excuse me.

279
00:14:01,419 --> 00:14:03,171
I was looking over your interview
with Agent Lisbon.

280
00:14:03,296 --> 00:14:04,672
You mentioned blood tests.

281
00:14:04,797 --> 00:14:08,134
When Amy and I went to our first modeling
gig, the nurse took our blood.

282
00:14:08,509 --> 00:14:10,928
They said we couldn't enter Europe
without a blood test.

283
00:14:11,220 --> 00:14:12,305
They lied.

284
00:14:14,098 --> 00:14:16,517
I'm thinking that mark
has something to do with your blood test.

285
00:14:16,642 --> 00:14:18,478
- Like how?
- We'll find out.

286
00:14:18,603 --> 00:14:20,521
You keep saying that, but nothing happens.

287
00:14:22,648 --> 00:14:24,400
Just keep looking over
those photos, okay?

288
00:14:35,703 --> 00:14:36,913
It's not your fault.

289
00:14:38,164 --> 00:14:41,751
It's all my fault.
I talked Amy into this.

290
00:14:55,681 --> 00:14:56,724
Hey, boss.

291
00:14:57,934 --> 00:14:59,936
- Any breaks in the case?
- Maybe.

292
00:15:00,061 --> 00:15:02,980
They took a sample of blood from all
the trafficking victims, including Daniela.

293
00:15:03,731 --> 00:15:06,192
Well, that's hardly standard procedure
for traffickers.

294
00:15:06,943 --> 00:15:09,028
- Why do you think they needed the blood?
- I don't know yet

295
00:15:09,153 --> 00:15:11,197
but I just went through
all the evidence from the house.

296
00:15:11,322 --> 00:15:14,325
I found these inside a first aid kit...
blood-test equipment.

297
00:15:14,450 --> 00:15:17,245
According to the Q.R. codes
they were supplied by a Dallas company.

298
00:15:17,370 --> 00:15:18,871
Rutherford Medical Supply.

299
00:15:18,996 --> 00:15:21,416
Well, their purchase records
can help find our traffickers.

300
00:15:21,958 --> 00:15:23,167
Good work.

301
00:15:24,168 --> 00:15:25,211
Hey, Cho.

302
00:15:26,087 --> 00:15:27,922
I know you're working with Daniela on this

303
00:15:28,589 --> 00:15:30,383
but try not to get her hopes up.

304
00:15:30,967 --> 00:15:34,345
The longer this stretches on the less likely
we'll be able to find her sister.

305
00:15:44,313 --> 00:15:45,356
It's nice.

306
00:15:46,149 --> 00:15:47,191
A little dark.

307
00:15:49,735 --> 00:15:50,987
I am Aurelio.

308
00:15:51,279 --> 00:15:54,115
- May I hang your coat?
- Yes. Thank you.

309
00:15:57,034 --> 00:15:58,202
This way, please.

310
00:16:00,538 --> 00:16:04,333
I have just the table
for your... special evening.

311
00:16:04,459 --> 00:16:06,878
- Oh, it's just dinner.
- Nonsense.

312
00:16:07,336 --> 00:16:10,715
Every night with this one
is, uh, an occasion, yes?

313
00:16:10,840 --> 00:16:12,049
Can't deny that.

314
00:16:13,593 --> 00:16:16,179
Good. Settle in.
I'll be right back.

315
00:16:19,265 --> 00:16:23,144
I owe you an apology
about the grand jury thing.

316
00:16:23,269 --> 00:16:27,064
I overreacted.
You're just doing what Abbott told you to.

317
00:16:27,732 --> 00:16:28,774
Oh.

318
00:16:29,275 --> 00:16:30,985
Did Marcus help you decide that?

319
00:16:31,360 --> 00:16:32,945
As a matter of fact...

320
00:16:33,070 --> 00:16:34,697
Right. Well, Marcus is wrong.

321
00:16:34,822 --> 00:16:38,117
I should've told you
and you were right to be cross with me.

322
00:16:39,619 --> 00:16:42,872
I pled the fifth, by the way,
in the grand jury room.

323
00:16:42,997 --> 00:16:44,123
Figured as much.

324
00:16:44,248 --> 00:16:46,375
You always know what I'm gonna do, huh?

325
00:16:46,501 --> 00:16:49,128
- Mostly.
- One of these days, I might surprise you.

326
00:16:49,253 --> 00:16:51,047
Please don't. I love that you're predictable.

327
00:16:51,172 --> 00:16:52,798
Just what a girl wants to hear.

328
00:16:53,341 --> 00:16:54,842
What does a girl wanna hear?

329
00:16:56,052 --> 00:16:57,386
I wish I knew.

330
00:17:00,139 --> 00:17:01,265
Your wine menu.

331
00:17:02,308 --> 00:17:03,935
- Thank you.
- If I may...

332
00:17:04,060 --> 00:17:07,480
we just imported a beautiful
Brunello di Montalcino.

333
00:17:08,189 --> 00:17:10,691
Well, uh, what do the mobsters drink?

334
00:17:12,485 --> 00:17:17,323
Yeah, the... the guidebook said that
real-life mobsters used to favor eating here.

335
00:17:19,575 --> 00:17:23,621
Yes. My father once
served Joseph Civello.

336
00:17:24,121 --> 00:17:27,833
Joseph Campisi
all the old Dallas mob families.

337
00:17:28,251 --> 00:17:31,254
They came here
for a taste of the old country.

338
00:17:31,879 --> 00:17:36,467
They used to say, "The veal is good
but the goodfellas... they're bad."

339
00:17:36,592 --> 00:17:37,927
What about now?

340
00:17:38,052 --> 00:17:41,722
Well, fortunately, there are no active
crime families left in Dallas.

341
00:17:41,847 --> 00:17:43,266
Well, isn't there that guy?
What's his name?

342
00:17:43,391 --> 00:17:46,352
The up-and-coming guy
John, uh, Acardo.

343
00:17:46,477 --> 00:17:48,354
He's, uh...
does he ever eat here?

344
00:17:49,146 --> 00:17:52,900
I wouldn't know anything about that.
I'll bring your server.

345
00:17:54,569 --> 00:17:55,736
Thank you.

346
00:17:56,946 --> 00:17:58,656
- It's Italian.
- You're good.

347
00:17:58,781 --> 00:18:00,700
- What do you think?
- I think I'd like to take a look

348
00:18:00,825 --> 00:18:02,785
at the evidence from the original case.

349
00:18:03,744 --> 00:18:07,456
And then I was thinking
about the Brunello di Montalcino.

350
00:18:07,582 --> 00:18:10,209
- That did sound good.
- 130 bucks a bottle.

351
00:18:10,585 --> 00:18:12,503
- Abbott's paying. Let's get it.
- Bingo.

352
00:18:16,882 --> 00:18:20,011
This is all the physical evidence
from the Elijah Macy murder scene.

353
00:18:20,136 --> 00:18:23,639
I sorted it by order of relevance with
this end of the table being most relevant.

354
00:18:23,764 --> 00:18:24,974
What do you consider relevant?

355
00:18:25,099 --> 00:18:27,476
Any object that can yield
trace evidence of the shooter.

356
00:18:27,602 --> 00:18:30,479
If we ID the triggerman, we can
cut a deal with him to finger Acardo.

357
00:18:30,605 --> 00:18:31,897
Yeah, uh, trace evidence

358
00:18:32,023 --> 00:18:34,275
that's the fibers and the hairs
and the gooey stuff, yeah?

359
00:18:34,400 --> 00:18:36,319
That's an oversimplification, but, yes.

360
00:18:36,444 --> 00:18:39,071
In the last year alone,
there are 17 new methods

361
00:18:39,196 --> 00:18:41,407
- for extracting DNA evidence from...
- Yeah, we know that there's a lotta ways

362
00:18:41,532 --> 00:18:43,868
- to find out what we need, right, Wylie?
- Yeah.

363
00:18:43,993 --> 00:18:46,412
So, uh, there's the federal way,
with the trace evidence

364
00:18:46,537 --> 00:18:49,123
and then there's a more interesting way.
Let's go with the more interesting way.

365
00:18:49,749 --> 00:18:51,626
Tell me, hmm?

366
00:18:51,751 --> 00:18:56,547
If I were to ask you what is the most random
item on this table, what would you say?

367
00:18:59,425 --> 00:19:01,385
This. An overcoat.

368
00:19:02,011 --> 00:19:04,055
It was found 200 feet
from the crime scene.

369
00:19:04,180 --> 00:19:05,848
There were no trace elements
found on it.

370
00:19:05,973 --> 00:19:08,225
The detectives weren't even sure
it was connected to the crime scene.

371
00:19:08,351 --> 00:19:10,186
What was the weather like
the day of the murder?

372
00:19:10,645 --> 00:19:15,149
- Pretty warm, if I'm not mistaken.
- Holes. Hmm.

373
00:19:15,608 --> 00:19:17,652
Interesting. You see that, Wylie?

374
00:19:17,943 --> 00:19:21,447
Okay, so, if this didn't belong
to the victim

375
00:19:21,572 --> 00:19:23,157
you have just solved the case.

376
00:19:24,075 --> 00:19:25,826
- I did?
- Well done.

377
00:19:26,369 --> 00:19:28,329
Find out if it belonged to the victim or not.

378
00:19:31,791 --> 00:19:33,084
Okay...

379
00:19:37,088 --> 00:19:38,881
- Mrs. Macy?
- Yes?

380
00:19:39,006 --> 00:19:43,094
Special Agent Fischer, FBI.
I apologize for the intrusion.

381
00:19:43,219 --> 00:19:44,595
I need your help.

382
00:19:45,304 --> 00:19:46,889
It's about your son.

383
00:19:48,808 --> 00:19:51,894
- Um... come on in.
- Thank you.

384
00:19:52,019 --> 00:19:56,482
When you raise a son alone
you don't stop and think about, well...

385
00:19:57,066 --> 00:19:59,944
Anything, really.
You're just trying to keep up.

386
00:20:00,569 --> 00:20:03,739
It wasn't until Elijah was, uh...

387
00:20:04,990 --> 00:20:10,246
taken away that I realized how woven he was
into the fabric of my entire life.

388
00:20:10,579 --> 00:20:11,789
Tell me about him.

389
00:20:13,374 --> 00:20:19,213
Elijah was such an inquisitive child
loved building things

390
00:20:19,338 --> 00:20:21,716
taught himself to code when he was 12.

391
00:20:21,841 --> 00:20:25,428
I understand he started his internet
company while he was still in college.

392
00:20:25,553 --> 00:20:28,389
That's right, junior year.
He was Plan II up at U.T.

393
00:20:28,514 --> 00:20:31,642
and I thought, "Oh, no.
He's just never gonna have the time."

394
00:20:31,767 --> 00:20:35,604
But he was so excited
to launch that website.

395
00:20:36,063 --> 00:20:37,314
In six months...

396
00:20:38,357 --> 00:20:40,401
paid off my house with the profits.

397
00:20:40,526 --> 00:20:42,027
Graduated with honors, too.

398
00:20:42,361 --> 00:20:43,696
Special kid.

399
00:20:44,822 --> 00:20:45,865
Yeah.

400
00:20:46,991 --> 00:20:49,326
If you'll allow me just one more question.

401
00:20:51,996 --> 00:20:54,999
Was this Elijah's Coat?

402
00:21:02,465 --> 00:21:05,176
According to his mother,
Elijah was allergic to wool

403
00:21:05,301 --> 00:21:08,179
and that coat is 100% wool
so it didn't belong to the victim.

404
00:21:08,763 --> 00:21:10,097
I was hoping you would say that.

405
00:21:10,222 --> 00:21:12,224
Well, I know how John Acardo
ordered the hit

406
00:21:12,349 --> 00:21:14,935
and, more importantly,
I know how to prove it.

407
00:21:15,060 --> 00:21:16,312
- How?
- It's easy.

408
00:21:16,437 --> 00:21:20,024
I just need to make
Acardo put a hit out on me.

409
00:21:52,095 --> 00:21:53,806
Acardo, 11 o'clock.

410
00:21:57,451 --> 00:22:00,329
You really think you can get him
to take out a hit on you in one night?

411
00:22:00,454 --> 00:22:02,456
Usually, only people who know you
wanna kill you.

412
00:22:03,232 --> 00:22:05,734
It's just a matter of figuring out
which buttons to push.

413
00:22:05,859 --> 00:22:10,030
A man like Acardo, you know,
he, uh, has a fierce need for control

414
00:22:10,155 --> 00:22:12,491
fancies himself the lord
of all he surveys.

415
00:22:12,741 --> 00:22:15,410
Elijah Macy cuts into his profits

416
00:22:15,536 --> 00:22:17,788
he responds by eliminating
the competition.

417
00:22:17,913 --> 00:22:20,874
A grand jury tries to indict him,
he buys the outcome.

418
00:22:20,999 --> 00:22:25,337
All I need to do is to take something
that belongs to him.

419
00:22:26,088 --> 00:22:27,297
Like what?

420
00:22:28,882 --> 00:22:30,259
What about her?

421
00:22:32,386 --> 00:22:34,555
Good luck with that.
I'll be at the bar.

422
00:22:47,943 --> 00:22:50,028
- Oh! Oh, God.
- I am so sorry.

423
00:22:50,487 --> 00:22:53,198
- Please excuse me.
- No problem. No problem.

424
00:22:53,323 --> 00:22:55,409
It's your arms. They're so sparkly.

425
00:22:55,659 --> 00:22:57,703
Oh, my God.
You noticed my glitter lotion?

426
00:22:57,828 --> 00:22:59,872
Yeah. Oh, look. It's on me, too.

427
00:22:59,997 --> 00:23:02,916
At least somebody did.
Wait, check it out in the light.

428
00:23:03,083 --> 00:23:05,460
- That is fantastic.
- Yeah, it's so shiny.

429
00:23:05,961 --> 00:23:07,421
- Oh, no.
- What is it? What's wrong?

430
00:23:07,754 --> 00:23:10,632
- What is it?
- Um... My... my bracelet.

431
00:23:10,757 --> 00:23:12,301
What does it look like?
Let me help you find it.

432
00:23:12,426 --> 00:23:14,219
- Let me find it.
- It-it's a diamond tennis bracelet.

433
00:23:14,344 --> 00:23:15,679
It's from my fiancé.

434
00:23:15,804 --> 00:23:18,181
- Your fiancé bought you this?
- Yes.

435
00:23:18,724 --> 00:23:20,392
- Thank you! Thank you!
- No, that's fine.

436
00:23:20,517 --> 00:23:21,768
- Thank you.
- Here, look.

437
00:23:22,269 --> 00:23:25,147
I mean, no offense
but I can't say much for his taste.

438
00:23:25,272 --> 00:23:29,026
I would put nothing more than Cartier
on your wrist around your neck

439
00:23:30,152 --> 00:23:31,445
or dangling from here.

440
00:23:36,116 --> 00:23:37,910
- You're so sweet.
- I'm Patrick.

441
00:23:38,368 --> 00:23:39,578
Brandy.

442
00:23:43,332 --> 00:23:44,833
What kinda scotch you got?

443
00:23:48,253 --> 00:23:50,672
Oh, look.

444
00:23:51,423 --> 00:23:53,008
Casablanca is on.

445
00:23:53,634 --> 00:23:55,427
Here's looking at you, kid.

446
00:23:56,011 --> 00:23:58,972
You know Bogart made that line up?
It wasn't in the script.

447
00:23:59,222 --> 00:24:00,807
I've never seen Casablanca.

448
00:24:00,933 --> 00:24:04,561
- What? Are you serious?
- I'm not really into old movies.

449
00:24:04,686 --> 00:24:06,772
This is not an old movie.

450
00:24:06,897 --> 00:24:09,024
This is a classic.
You've gotta watch this.

451
00:24:09,149 --> 00:24:11,526
- You're gonna love it.
- Okay, let's watch it.

452
00:24:13,987 --> 00:24:15,155
Is everything okay?

453
00:24:15,489 --> 00:24:16,865
Yeah, I'm just tired.

454
00:24:17,783 --> 00:24:19,368
Well, Teresa...

455
00:24:19,826 --> 00:24:21,787
I may not be able to read minds
like Jane

456
00:24:21,912 --> 00:24:23,956
but even I can tell
when something's going on.

457
00:24:24,081 --> 00:24:26,833
Jane can't read minds.
It's hard to explain.

458
00:24:26,959 --> 00:24:28,418
You know, it doesn't matter.

459
00:24:31,088 --> 00:24:34,091
- Do you wanna talk about it?
- Not really, no.

460
00:24:43,725 --> 00:24:48,438
I just wanted to say that I know
moving to D.C. is a big thing

461
00:24:48,563 --> 00:24:55,821
and... it might raise issues you don't feel
totally comfortable talking to me about.

462
00:24:57,656 --> 00:24:59,449
But I just want you to know it's okay.

463
00:25:01,284 --> 00:25:03,704
I mean, you go through
whatever process you need to, just...

464
00:25:06,331 --> 00:25:08,166
let me know when you make a decision.

465
00:25:12,379 --> 00:25:13,630
I'll be here.

466
00:25:16,049 --> 00:25:17,175
Thank you.

467
00:25:24,391 --> 00:25:26,393
Let's watch Casablanca, huh?

468
00:25:29,563 --> 00:25:31,023
What is it about, anyway?

469
00:25:31,398 --> 00:25:34,192
Well, various things
but basically it's a love story

470
00:25:34,317 --> 00:25:37,154
about a woman who has to choose
between two men.

471
00:25:41,074 --> 00:25:42,784
There's also a baseball game on.

472
00:25:42,909 --> 00:25:45,162
- That sounds better.
- Yeah, let's do that.

473
00:25:53,045 --> 00:25:54,838
Hey, boss, check this guy out.

474
00:26:07,309 --> 00:26:10,604
- Brandy.
- Dance card's full for the night, friend.

475
00:26:15,025 --> 00:26:17,027
This can go one of two ways.

476
00:26:17,527 --> 00:26:21,281
You can take your hands off her body
or I can cut 'em off.

477
00:26:22,699 --> 00:26:24,117
You must be the fiancé.

478
00:26:24,242 --> 00:26:26,870
Let me tell you something, pal.
You are marrying a wonderful dancer.

479
00:26:26,995 --> 00:26:29,372
It's okay, baby. Nothing to worry about.
Look.

480
00:26:29,831 --> 00:26:31,458
- He found my bracelet.
- Found the bracelet.

481
00:26:31,583 --> 00:26:32,959
- Oh, did he?
- Uh-huh.

482
00:26:33,085 --> 00:26:34,961
- What did you say your name was?
- Uh, this is Patrick.

483
00:26:35,087 --> 00:26:36,963
Jane. Patrick Jane.

484
00:26:37,339 --> 00:26:40,050
Well, Patrick, I think it's time for you
to say good night.

485
00:26:40,258 --> 00:26:42,010
- On your way.
- Oh, no, not yet.

486
00:26:42,135 --> 00:26:43,929
This, this may be my last night
of freedom.

487
00:26:44,054 --> 00:26:47,724
I'm being indicted for murder
and I was hoping to go out big, you know?

488
00:26:47,849 --> 00:26:50,143
Finest champagne, finest woman.

489
00:26:50,268 --> 00:26:52,562
Brandy's spending the night with me.
I hope you don't mind.

490
00:26:52,687 --> 00:26:54,981
- Oh, he's just joking, Johnny.
- Oh, no, I'm not.

491
00:26:55,107 --> 00:26:57,150
And I'm gonna take you places
that you've never been.

492
00:26:57,275 --> 00:26:59,736
And when you get back
you can tell Johnny all about it.

493
00:26:59,861 --> 00:27:01,905
That'll be my wedding gift
to the two of you.

494
00:27:02,030 --> 00:27:04,825
I think our friend Patrick here
has had a little too much to drink.

495
00:27:04,950 --> 00:27:06,743
- Why don't you escort him out?
- Excuse me!

496
00:27:06,868 --> 00:27:09,204
Can I have your attention, please
ladies and gentlemen?

497
00:27:09,329 --> 00:27:14,543
That man, John Acardo
is a drug dealer and killer.

498
00:27:15,210 --> 00:27:17,337
I want you all to remember his face

499
00:27:17,462 --> 00:27:19,589
and the faces of his men here

500
00:27:19,714 --> 00:27:22,300
so that you can describe them
later to the police.

501
00:27:23,343 --> 00:27:26,138
They're gonna physically assault me
or worse...

502
00:27:26,263 --> 00:27:28,056
the moment I walk out that door.

503
00:27:30,433 --> 00:27:33,103
Thank you and good night.

504
00:27:45,866 --> 00:27:49,202
Arrange a dinner tomorrow night.
Il Tavolo Bianco.

505
00:27:49,327 --> 00:27:50,579
My usual table.

506
00:27:52,539 --> 00:27:54,583
Hey. Careful.

507
00:27:56,001 --> 00:27:57,419
Get over here!

508
00:28:10,682 --> 00:28:12,934
"The question isn't what medical
equipment do we supply."

509
00:28:13,059 --> 00:28:16,021
It's what don't we supply.
Gelatin capsules, glass vials

510
00:28:16,146 --> 00:28:18,148
hospital beds
and everything in between.

511
00:28:18,273 --> 00:28:20,108
- Put that in the corner, Bob.
- "Okay."

512
00:28:20,233 --> 00:28:21,484
What about blood-test strips?

513
00:28:21,985 --> 00:28:23,236
Did these come from Rutherford Medical?

514
00:28:23,361 --> 00:28:25,405
Uh, yeah, these are
from our home-typing kits.

515
00:28:25,530 --> 00:28:26,740
Do you also handle the results?

516
00:28:26,865 --> 00:28:28,867
No, this particular strip is from a home kit

517
00:28:28,992 --> 00:28:30,994
so there's no need
to send in for a lab report.

518
00:28:31,745 --> 00:28:32,871
"Bow and arrow.'

519
00:28:33,163 --> 00:28:34,664
- Agent Cho.
- Yeah.

520
00:28:36,791 --> 00:28:38,001
It's a bow and arrow.

521
00:28:38,585 --> 00:28:39,878
The first trafficking victim
that Jane found...

522
00:28:40,003 --> 00:28:41,671
Didn't she say something
about a bow and arrow?

523
00:28:41,796 --> 00:28:42,839
Just before she died.

524
00:28:42,964 --> 00:28:45,133
That's a blood-type kit
from our Arquero line.

525
00:28:45,258 --> 00:28:46,468
That's one of our best sellers.

526
00:28:46,593 --> 00:28:49,054
We're gonna need a printout of everyone
who ordered one of these kits.

527
00:28:49,179 --> 00:28:50,388
That's a long list.

528
00:28:50,513 --> 00:28:53,183
We sell those to half of the hospitals
and clinics in the state.

529
00:28:53,308 --> 00:28:54,351
We'll wait.

530
00:28:55,852 --> 00:28:56,978
Whatever.

531
00:29:07,113 --> 00:29:08,156
Mr. Acardo.

532
00:29:10,075 --> 00:29:12,285
And how is my Bella Donna tonight?

533
00:29:12,410 --> 00:29:14,496
Aurelio, you always make be blush.

534
00:29:15,330 --> 00:29:16,998
Your, uh, usual table is waiting.

535
00:29:17,123 --> 00:29:18,750
- Excellent.
- May I hang your coat?

536
00:29:19,960 --> 00:29:23,922
Of course. And, uh, take
Nico's coat, too, would ya?

537
00:29:24,464 --> 00:29:25,590
Of course.

538
00:29:26,841 --> 00:29:30,428
Marie, show Mr. Acardo
to his table, eh?

539
00:29:41,815 --> 00:29:43,942
Take good care of Mr. Acardo, eh?

540
00:29:54,452 --> 00:29:57,622
Johnny, how do you think
I'd look in a Cartier necklace?

541
00:29:59,332 --> 00:30:01,334
Uh, no need, Alfons.
Um...

542
00:30:01,459 --> 00:30:03,295
I think I'll have
a New York strip tonight...

543
00:30:04,170 --> 00:30:07,299
Rare.
And, uh, salad? Is that good?

544
00:30:07,424 --> 00:30:08,633
A salad for her.

545
00:30:09,342 --> 00:30:11,219
- Yeah.
- Thank you, Alfons.

546
00:31:12,906 --> 00:31:13,990
It's me.

547
00:31:16,368 --> 00:31:17,577
I've got it.

548
00:31:22,415 --> 00:31:23,708
Drop it on the ground.

549
00:31:27,921 --> 00:31:29,172
Now come here.

550
00:31:34,886 --> 00:31:36,179
You know what to do.

551
00:31:52,821 --> 00:31:54,864
Drop the weapon.
Drop it now.

552
00:31:55,573 --> 00:31:57,909
Don't shoot!
I am unarmed.

553
00:31:58,034 --> 00:32:01,246
- Put the gun down!
- Okay. Be calm.

554
00:32:03,498 --> 00:32:06,918
God. Hey.
Fischer. Can you breathe?

555
00:32:07,043 --> 00:32:08,169
I'm okay.

556
00:32:09,087 --> 00:32:10,255
Check the shooter.

557
00:32:11,089 --> 00:32:13,842
- I heard shooting. Everyone all right?
- Oh, yeah.

558
00:32:18,513 --> 00:32:23,893
- He's dead.
- Ah. This is how Acardo orders his hits.

559
00:32:24,018 --> 00:32:27,188
He sends a message through Aurelio
to distance himself from the gunman.

560
00:32:27,313 --> 00:32:30,108
They made me.
They made me do this.

561
00:32:30,233 --> 00:32:32,026
Calm down! Put your hands
on your head.

562
00:32:32,152 --> 00:32:35,530
And then Acardo puts the target's name
in the pocket of the jacket.

563
00:32:37,407 --> 00:32:38,575
Patrick Jane.

564
00:32:40,743 --> 00:32:41,786
That's me.

565
00:32:54,105 --> 00:32:57,066
Excuse me.
I think this, uh, belongs to you.

566
00:32:57,191 --> 00:32:58,901
Sit down and put your hands
on the table.

567
00:32:59,908 --> 00:33:00,992
And this.

568
00:33:01,821 --> 00:33:03,906
- And this.
- Oh, my God! He's choking!

569
00:33:04,031 --> 00:33:06,450
He's choking!
Oh, my God! He's choking!

570
00:33:06,851 --> 00:33:09,645
- Do something! Do something!
- Here we go. Ready?

571
00:33:10,062 --> 00:33:11,397
One, two, three.

572
00:33:15,818 --> 00:33:17,403
There we go, huh?

573
00:33:17,904 --> 00:33:20,698
- All better and off to prison.
- Let's go. Come on.

574
00:33:21,032 --> 00:33:22,992
Oh, God.
Oh, God.

575
00:33:24,494 --> 00:33:25,953
Oh, thank you.

576
00:33:28,164 --> 00:33:29,290
Um...

577
00:33:29,415 --> 00:33:31,042
- Choking?
- No, I'm good.

578
00:33:36,047 --> 00:33:37,340
Hey, Jane.

579
00:33:38,508 --> 00:33:39,717
Did you hear the news?

580
00:33:40,092 --> 00:33:42,136
Your grand jury investigation ended.

581
00:33:42,386 --> 00:33:44,430
And they voted to indict you for murder.

582
00:33:44,680 --> 00:33:47,725
- Well, is it any wonder?
- Well, the AG is making it go away

583
00:33:47,850 --> 00:33:50,478
but, you know, it's a good thing
that you're under our protection.

584
00:33:51,437 --> 00:33:53,773
That's a fact
that you'll never let me forget.

585
00:33:54,857 --> 00:33:56,317
How was your dinner with Lisbon?

586
00:33:56,442 --> 00:33:59,403
Oh, my chicken was so-so,
but Lisbon's fish was excellent.

587
00:33:59,529 --> 00:34:01,822
- Why do you ask?
- Just wondering.

588
00:34:12,542 --> 00:34:13,709
Lydia Faulk.

589
00:34:15,545 --> 00:34:16,712
Agent Fischer.

590
00:34:16,837 --> 00:34:19,423
We didn't recall you to testify. I'll let you
know if we need you to come back in.

591
00:34:19,549 --> 00:34:22,635
Oh, that won't be necessary.
The case against Jane was a setup.

592
00:34:23,094 --> 00:34:26,222
- What are you talking about?
- A sting to net a corrupt prosecutor.

593
00:34:26,847 --> 00:34:29,767
You're under arrest
for grand jury tampering and bribery.

594
00:34:29,892 --> 00:34:32,853
- Bailiff, take these people out.
- Arrest warrant. Back off.

595
00:34:33,980 --> 00:34:35,856
We have Acardo's phone.

596
00:34:35,982 --> 00:34:37,733
The two of you had a lot to say
to each other

597
00:34:37,858 --> 00:34:40,736
the night he received his subpoena
to appear before the grand jury.

598
00:34:40,861 --> 00:34:44,615
And then there's the large deposit
into your offshore account two days later.

599
00:34:46,367 --> 00:34:47,577
Cuff her.

600
00:34:57,169 --> 00:34:58,504
Okay, well...

601
00:34:59,171 --> 00:35:00,715
We have good news for you.

602
00:35:01,215 --> 00:35:05,511
The Attorney General agreed not to
prosecute if you testify against Acardo.

603
00:35:06,846 --> 00:35:10,641
Acardo came to me
and said he'd make my wife a widow

604
00:35:11,267 --> 00:35:13,185
if I didn't do what he ordered, eh?

605
00:35:13,519 --> 00:35:16,522
You will be moved
to protective custody, of course.

606
00:35:18,399 --> 00:35:20,401
And what about Il Tavolo Bianco, eh?

607
00:35:28,034 --> 00:35:29,535
I testify for you.

608
00:35:31,078 --> 00:35:32,413
But I won't leave my home

609
00:35:33,664 --> 00:35:35,082
or my restaurant.

610
00:35:36,000 --> 00:35:37,918
If Acardo wants revenge

611
00:35:38,461 --> 00:35:40,087
I'll stay out in the open

612
00:35:41,172 --> 00:35:42,840
where they can find me.

613
00:35:43,716 --> 00:35:46,427
Otherwise, they hurt my family
to find me.

614
00:35:46,886 --> 00:35:47,970
Okay.

615
00:35:48,763 --> 00:35:52,600
Well, you will have to remain
in protective custody until the trial.

616
00:35:54,226 --> 00:35:55,394
I understand.

617
00:35:55,519 --> 00:35:57,480
I'll send an agent in
to walk you through that.

618
00:35:58,731 --> 00:35:59,940
Good luck.

619
00:36:03,152 --> 00:36:04,904
You made a tough choice, Mr. Aurelio.

620
00:36:06,405 --> 00:36:09,075
Brave man.
That's very admirable.

621
00:36:29,220 --> 00:36:30,596
Agent Cho.

622
00:36:32,139 --> 00:36:33,182
What am I looking at?

623
00:36:33,307 --> 00:36:36,435
Uh, I cross-referenced a list of facilities
that purchased the Arquero kits

624
00:36:36,560 --> 00:36:38,813
with their location
and last date of purchase.

625
00:36:38,938 --> 00:36:40,773
- What am I looking at?
- Oh, well, if...

626
00:36:40,898 --> 00:36:42,525
if you'll notice the highlighted line

627
00:36:42,650 --> 00:36:45,611
20 kits were delivered to Wheatondale
Medical Center last month.

628
00:36:45,945 --> 00:36:47,697
- Okay?
- Wheatondale isn't in operation.

629
00:36:47,822 --> 00:36:49,365
It was condemned two years ago.

630
00:37:19,770 --> 00:37:21,063
Let's try the other door.

631
00:37:39,665 --> 00:37:40,708
Let's break it.

632
00:38:17,578 --> 00:38:18,621
You hear that?

633
00:38:19,580 --> 00:38:22,333
That humming sound?
Sounds like a generator.

634
00:38:24,084 --> 00:38:25,461
It's coming from in there.

635
00:38:40,476 --> 00:38:42,019
Someone's been using
this place.

636
00:38:42,478 --> 00:38:44,021
Yeah, but for what?

637
00:38:44,897 --> 00:38:46,273
The hum is coming from in there.

638
00:39:11,131 --> 00:39:13,092
What? What is it?

639
00:39:14,844 --> 00:39:16,220
The body's been cut open.

640
00:39:16,887 --> 00:39:18,222
Looks like they took the heart.

641
00:39:23,811 --> 00:39:25,437
They're killing these girls
for their organs.

642
00:39:45,916 --> 00:39:47,084
Jane.

643
00:39:48,085 --> 00:39:49,169
Lisbon in?

644
00:39:50,254 --> 00:39:51,297
Yeah.

645
00:39:51,422 --> 00:39:54,884
Teresa. It's, uh...
it's Jane.

646
00:40:01,181 --> 00:40:02,975
- What's up?
- Cannoli...

647
00:40:03,517 --> 00:40:07,563
Il Tavolo Bianco.
Mrs. Aurelio made them specially.

648
00:40:07,688 --> 00:40:08,731
- That's sweet.
- Yeah.

649
00:40:08,856 --> 00:40:10,858
There's, there's enough there
for Marcus, too.

650
00:40:13,277 --> 00:40:16,906
Jane. You didn't come here this late
to drop off cannoli.

651
00:40:28,292 --> 00:40:31,128
Look, I-I've been thinking
about you leaving.

652
00:40:31,962 --> 00:40:33,005
And...

653
00:40:34,715 --> 00:40:38,177
I want you to know
that I-I really want you to be happy.

654
00:40:38,469 --> 00:40:40,387
And that is the most important
thing to me...

655
00:40:40,512 --> 00:40:42,431
That you do what makes you happy.

656
00:40:46,602 --> 00:40:48,145
- Okay?
- Yeah.

